GMB POST 1 - PROBLEM ANGLE:

It's the King's Birthday long weekend, and gyms across the Melbourne CBD are about to get a lot busier. A good number of those people haven't lifted a weight since last winter.

Here's the pattern we see every year about now. As Melbourne's mornings turn cold, runners who've spent autumn on the Tan or the Yarra trail decide to move indoors and pick up roughly where they left off twelve months ago, often with the same weights and the same number of sessions, on a body that's spent the year adjusting to desk work and outdoor running instead of the gym floor.

Add a long weekend, where three or four sessions get squeezed into a few days instead of spread across a full week, and the body simply hasn't had time to adjust. Tight hips from sitting, rounded shoulders from screen time, and cold muscles meeting an unfamiliar load are a common combination behind the lower back and shoulder strains MOSIC's Bourke Street and Essendon clinics see in the days after a long weekend.

